// Original study questions based on Andriy Burkov, 2019.
$CATEGORY: Chapter 2 — Notation and Definitions

::Data structures::Match each mathematical object to its description. {
    =Scalar -> One numerical value
    =Vector -> An ordered list of numerical values
    =Matrix -> A rectangular array of numerical values
    =Set -> An unordered collection of unique elements
    ####Ordering matters for vectors, but not for sets; repeated elements do not increase set cardinality. Source\: section 2.1.1.
}

::Indexed feature notation::What does x_i^(j) denote in the book? {
    ~Example j raised to the power i
    ~The j-th power of the whole dataset
    =Feature j of example i
    ~The number of examples multiplied by the number of features
    ####The parenthesized superscript is an index, not an exponent. Source\: section 2.1.1.
}

::Sigma::Compute the sum of 2i for i from 1 through 4. {
    #20:0
    ####2 + 4 + 6 + 8 \= 20. Source\: section 2.1.2.
}

::Pi::Compute the product of i for i from 1 through 4. {
    #24:0
    ####1 * 2 * 3 * 4 \= 24. Source\: section 2.1.3.
}

::Set operations::Let A contain 1, 3, 5 and B contain 3, 4. Which is their intersection? {
    ~The set containing 1, 3, 4, 5
    =The set containing only 3
    ~The ordered list 1, 3, 5, 3, 4
    ~The empty set
    ####Intersection retains elements present in both sets; union would contain 1, 3, 4, 5. Source\: section 2.1.4.
}

::Derived set::Let S contain 1, 3, 4, 5. Form T from x squared for x in S with x > 3. What is the sum of the elements of T? {
    #41:0
    ####Only 4 and 5 satisfy the condition; T contains 16 and 25, whose sum is 41. Source\: section 2.1.4.
}

::Dot product::Compute the dot product of [2, -1, 3] and [4, 5, 0]. {
    #3:0
    ####2*4 + (-1)*5 + 3*0 \= 3. A dot product is a scalar. Source\: section 2.1.5.
}

::Matrix-vector shape::W has 3 rows and 2 columns. x is a column vector with 2 entries. What is the shape of Wx? {
    ~A 2-by-3 matrix
    =A column vector with 3 entries
    ~A column vector with 2 entries
    ~The product is undefined
    ####The inner dimensions match, and each row of W produces one output entry. Source\: section 2.1.5.
}

::Arg max::For inputs a, b, c, a function returns 2, 7, 4 respectively. What does arg max return? {
    ~The sum 13
    ~7
    =b
    ~c
    ####arg max returns a maximizing input; max returns the output value 7. Source\: section 2.1.7.
}

::Local versus global::Which statement about a local minimum is correct? {
    =It is no higher than nearby values, but a lower value may exist elsewhere
    ~It must be the unique global minimum
    ~It must have a strictly positive derivative
    ~It is always higher than every neighboring value
    ####Local optimality concerns a neighborhood, while global optimality concerns the full domain. Source\: section 2.1.6.
}

::Chain rule::For F(x) \= (3x + 1)^2, what is F'(2)? {
    #42:0
    ####F'(x) \= 2(3x+1)*3, so F'(2) \= 42. Source\: section 2.1.9.
}

::Gradient::For f(u,v) \= 3u + 2v + 7, what is the gradient with coordinates ordered u, v? {
    ~7
    =[3, 2]
    ~[u, v]
    ~[3u, 2v]
    ####Take one partial derivative per coordinate, holding the other coordinate constant. Source\: section 2.1.9.
}

::Density versus probability::A random variable has a continuous density. Which statement is correct? {
    ~The density height at x is always the probability of exactly x
    =An interval probability is an area under the density; a single point has probability zero
    ~A density must be at most one at every point
    ~All continuous distributions assign positive probability to every individual point
    ####A density is not a probability mass function. Its integral is one; its height can exceed one. Source\: section 2.2.
}

::Expectation::X takes value 0 with probability 0.25 and value 4 with probability 0.75. What is E[X]? {
    #3:0
    ####The probability-weighted average is 0*0.25 + 4*0.75 \= 3. Source\: section 2.2.
}

::Variance::X takes 0 and 2, each with probability 0.5. What is its variance? {
    #1:0
    ####Its mean is 1, so variance is 0.5*(0-1)^2 + 0.5*(2-1)^2 \= 1. Source\: section 2.2.
}

::Unbiased does not mean exact::What does it mean for an estimator of a quantity to be unbiased? {
    ~It has zero variance
    =Its expectation over repeated samples equals the true quantity
    ~It never uses random samples
    ~Its value equals the true quantity for every possible sample
    ####An individual estimate can be wrong even when the estimator is unbiased. Source\: section 2.3.
}

::Sample mean::For the observed sample 2, 4, 9, what is the sample mean? {
    #5:0
    ####(2+4+9)/3 \= 5; the sample mean is an unbiased estimator of the population mean under the usual sampling assumptions. Source\: section 2.3.
}

::Bayes calculation::P(A) \= 0.2, P(B given A) \= 0.6, and P(B) \= 0.3. What is P(A given B)? {
    #0.4:0
    ####Bayes' rule gives 0.6*0.2/0.3 \= 0.4. Source\: section 2.4.
}

::Bayesian terms::Match each Bayesian term to its meaning. {
    =Prior -> Belief about parameters before the current data
    =Likelihood -> Data probability or density as a function of parameters
    =Posterior -> Belief about parameters after conditioning on data
    =MAP estimate -> Parameter value maximizing the posterior
    ####Posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior; evidence normalizes it. Source\: section 2.5.
}

::Parameters and hyperparameters::Select TWO hyperparameters rather than fitted model coefficients. {
    ~%-50%A fitted linear-regression coefficient
    ~%50%The chosen maximum depth of a decision tree
    ~%50%The chosen k in k-nearest neighbors
    ~%-50%A fitted linear-regression intercept
    ####Hyperparameters control the learning setup; coefficients and intercept are fitted within that setup. Hyperparameters can themselves be selected using validation. Source\: section 2.6.
}

::Prediction targets::Match each target type to the appropriate task. {
    =Spam or not spam -> Binary classification
    =One of cat, dog, horse -> Multiclass classification
    =A house price in euros -> Regression
    =One of three numeric class codes -> Multiclass classification, despite numeric encoding
    ####Task type depends on target meaning, not whether its storage format is numeric. Source\: section 2.7.
}

::Instance-based learning::Why does ordinary kNN need the training examples when predicting? {
    ~It requires the true target of the query before searching
    ~It cannot use a distance function
    ~It fits a fixed coefficient vector and discards all examples
    =Its predictions depend on finding nearby stored examples and their labels
    ####The stored examples are central to its instance-based prediction procedure. Source\: section 2.8.
}

::Deep representation::What distinguishes the deep-learning description in this chapter? {
    ~Any dataset with more than two rows
    =Multiple learned layers, with later layers receiving representations produced by preceding layers
    ~Any model trained for a large number of epochs
    ~Using a vector rather than a scalar input
    ####Depth concerns successive learned transformations, not merely dataset size or training duration. Source\: section 2.9.
}

::Bayesian sequential update::Explain how to update beliefs about a fixed parameter after two conditionally independent observations. {
    ####Self-assessment\: mark correct only if your answer includes ALL of the following. Start with a prior; multiply by the first observation's likelihood and normalize; use that posterior as the next prior; multiply by the second likelihood and normalize. Equivalently the final posterior is proportional to the original prior times both likelihoods. Do not count the prior twice. Source\: section 2.5.
}
